<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Fe, New Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Fe was founded around 1610 by the Spanish governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-peralta">Pedro de Peralta</a> as the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, on the northern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Pueblo rebels drove the Spanish out in 1680, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> reoccupied it in 1692. It remained the capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-fe-new-mexico.jpg" alt="Santa Fe, New Mexico" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tony Hisgett from Birmingham, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_the_Governors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Santa Fe lies at the foothills of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains and is the highest state capital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Its climate is cold semi-arid, with cool, dry winters and hot summers. The name is Spanish for Holy Faith and is a shortened form of the original La Villa Real de la Santa Fe de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> de Asís. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewa">Tewa</a> call the place P'oegeh, water place, a name that predates European colonisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was originally occupied by Tanoan peoples who lived in Pueblo villages along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>. Tewa built a cluster of homes around the site of today's Plaza sometime after 900 CE, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> had abandoned the site for at least 200 years by the time the Spanish arrived in the early 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> led the first Spanish effort to colonise the region in 1598; the Spanish banished him after his rule was judged cruel to the indigenous population.</p><p>Santa Fe was founded in 1610 as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Nuevo México</a>, a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, and is the oldest state capital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Spanish laid it out by the Laws of the Indies of 1573, around a central plaza with the Palace of the Governors on its north side. It prospered on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-real-de-tierra-adentro">Camino Real de Tierra Adentro</a> and the Santa Fe Trail. In 1846 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-w-kearny">Stephen W. Kearny</a> led some 1,700 soldiers into the city, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> in 1848 the United States gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. Santa Fe became the capital of the territory in 1851 and of the state in 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Santa Fe is the fourth most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and the county seat of Santa Fe County. It blends indigenous, Spanish and American influences and has been a UNESCO Creative City in Crafts and Folk Art since 2005. The Plaza and the Palace of the Governors remain at its centre, and its adobe-style Pueblo Revival and Territorial Revival architecture is largely preserved. The city has been associated with science since 1943, when it served as the gateway to Los Alamos National Laboratory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santa Fe, New Mexico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1610 governor Pedro de Peralta founded <b>Santa Fe</b> as the new capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
